WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.720
Since his debut in 1962, the Mighty Thor has
been one of the Marvel Universe's most powerful

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.029
heroes.

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:12.120
Even if you're caught up on his adventures,
can recite the inscription on his hammer from

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.850
memory, and know the name of the magical metal
it was crafted from, here are a few things

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.800
about Odin's favorite son that you'd be surprised
to learn.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.600
(when you try to go back and rewatch Thor:
The Dark World)

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.960
Kirby's Three Thors

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.300
When Stan Lee and Jack Kirby were creating
the Marvel Universe in the ‘60s, they’d

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.890
already been working in comics for 20 years.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:39.260
With that in mind, it might not be surprising
that Journey Into Mystery #83 wasn't Kirby's

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.390
first shot at doing a story about the Norse
god of Thunder.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.350
That honor goes to Adventure Comics #75.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:52.030
In a story by Kirby and his Captain America
collaborator Joe Simon, the Golden Age Sandman

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:57.010
goes up against a crook named "Fairy Tale"
Fenton, who masquerades as the hammer-swinging

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.650
thunder god in order to pull off daring robberies.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:05.230
After injuring the Sandman's sidekick and
sparking a full-on riot, Fenton is finally

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.250
brought down and sent packing, never to be
seen again.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:14.970
15 years later, Kirby would hit a little closer
to the mark with Tales of the Unexpected #16.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:20.030
A six-page story called "The Magic Hammer"
featured a Thor with plenty of familiar design

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:23.930
elements and a version of Mjolnir that looks
an awful lot like the one that would show

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:25.940
up at Marvel at the start of the next decade.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:31.100
It was even hidden on Earth until it was found
by a mere mortal who could tap into its power,

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.450
although in this case, it was discovered by
a cowboy crook named Bard who was anything

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:36.450
but worthy.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.060
The real twist, though?

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.840
While Kirby's third shot at Thor would be
a success for the fledgling Marvel Comics,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.230
both the Sandman story and Tales of the Unexpected
were published by DC.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.100
Journey Into Mystery

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.530
Building off the success that they'd found
by introducing the Fantastic Four the previous

00:01:54.530 --> 00:02:00.600
year, Lee and Kirby decided in 1962 to expand
their superhero universe, and the mighty Thor

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.550
charged into the pages of Journey Into Mystery
#83.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:09.710
For the most part, "The Stone Men From Saturn"
follows the usual beats of the sci-fi monster

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:14.560
comics that that Marvel had been publishing
in the ‘50s: aliens invade Earth, but get

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.460
scared off when they encounter someone who's
strong enough to stand against them.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:24.180
The big difference is that the "someone" is,
of course, Dr. Don Blake, who finds a cane

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.780
tucked away in a cave in Norway that can transform
him into Thor.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.540
But there’s another important character
who made his debut in that issue.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:38.610
In 2006, the "Planet Hulk" storyline revealed
that one of those "Stone Men From Saturn"

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:44.710
fled back to the stars and wound up stranded
on the planet Sakaar, living as a gladiator.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.931
His name was Korg, and while a similar rock
monster appeared in the opening of Thor: The

00:02:48.931 --> 00:02:53.920
Dark World in a tribute to that first issue,
Korg himself would wind up being a pretty

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.790
major character in Thor: Ragnarok.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:00.860
"P--- off, ghost!"

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.319
Dr. Don Who?

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:10.950
Marvel superheroes are often defined by having
disadvantages in their civilian identities

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.740
that bleed into their heroic exploits.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.180
Iron Man had to wear the chestplate of his
armor all the time in order to keep shrapnel

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.010
from moving closer to his heart.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.350
Spider-Man was perpetually broke and struggled
with school.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:28.340
For Thor, the consequence of losing control
of his hammer for 60 seconds was that he would

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:33.850
turn back into his human identity: Dr. Donald
Blake, a disabled doctor who worked with his

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.090
nurse and love interest, Jane Foster.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:42.000
In the ‘80s, Marvel introduced a big twist:
Don Blake didn't actually exist.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.459
Instead, Thor's human form was created by
Odin in order to teach his son the folly of

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:48.510
his arrogance.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:53.380
By imprisoning him in the body of a human,
Odin hoped that Thor would stop bragging and

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.770
learn to put the needs of others before his
own.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:04:00.420
Considering the stories we've gotten since,
it's safe to say that it worked.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.310
Tales of Asgard

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:07.090
While Thor himself was in the spotlight, Lee
and Kirby added an awful lot to Marvel's version

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:11.290
of Norse pantheon to help distinguish it from
the classic mythology.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:16.060
The "Tales of Asgard" backup stories adapted
myths and helped introduce new characters

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:21.410
like Fandral the Dashing, Hogun the Grim,
and Volstagg the Voluminous better known as

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.600
the Warriors Three.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.980
"Never cared for spies…"

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.200
"Exquisite."

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.760
Everything you need to know about those three
characters is right there in their names.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.150
Fandral is a swashbuckler based on the on-screen
exploits of Robin Hood actor Errol Flynn,

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:46.210
while Hogun was a fearsome fighter that, according
to Lee, was based on Charles Bronson.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:52.830
Volstagg was inspired by Falstaff, from Shakespeare's
Henry the Fourth: an aging warrior who often

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:57.950
exaggerated his own accomplishments, and was
eventually revealed to have an equally voluminous

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.500
wife and about a dozen kids.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.100
The Warriors Three quickly became fan favorite
supporting characters, and even managed to

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:10.139
spin off into a few stories focused entirely
on them as a leading trio.

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:11.970
The Thor Corps

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:18.130
When Walt Simonson took over Thor in 1983
for a truly definitive run on the character,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:22.150
one of the biggest changes he made was the
introduction of Beta Ray Bill.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:27.150
An alien "everyman" who looked like a cross
between an orange humanoid and a skeletal

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:31.650
horse, Bill was the chosen defender of his
race as they fled the destruction of their

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:32.840
home planet.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.699
After a cosmic misunderstanding led him into
a brutal battle against Thor, he proved he

00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:43.550
was worthy to hoist the hammer, and Odin granted
him one of his own: Stormbreaker.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:48.270
Bill wasn't the only other Marvel character
to ever wield the power of Thor.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:54.490
In 1988,Tom DeFalco and Ron Frenz gave Thor
a new secret identity in the form of Eric

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:59.930
Masterson, an architect whose inexperience
with godly powers gave him a pretty big challenge

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:01.360
in crime-fighting.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:08.150
In 1993, the two characters were separated,
but Eric continued his heroic career as Thunderstrike,

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:11.229
wielding a magical mace with the same name.

00:06:11.229 --> 00:06:18.820
In Thor #384, they introduced Dargo Ktor,
a resident of a 26th century dystopia who

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:22.660
lifted Mjolnir, and became that era's version
of Thor.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:27.510
Needless to say, he crossed over to the core
Marvel Universe to battle the Fantastic Four

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:30.260
on the orders of Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.750
No, really, that is the actual name of a Marvel
character.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:38.290
With all these Thors running around at the
same time, they obviously had to form a team.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:44.670
Thus was born the Thor Corps, in which Thor,
Beta Ray Bill, Thunderstrike, and Dargo united

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:49.510
for a battle across time and space that would
basically just prove how wild comics in the

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:51.639
'90s could be.

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:53.750
The Incredible Hulk Returns

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:59.540
Until the launch of the Marvel Cinematic Universe,
the Incredible Hulk TV show was one of Marvel's

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:01.440
greatest mass media successes.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.490
It ran for five seasons and gave the Hulk
one of the most memorable catchphrases of

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:07.490
all time.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.310
"Don’t make me angry.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:10.600
You wouldn't like me when I'm angry."

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:15.449
When Bill Bixby and Lou Ferrigno returned
as Banner and his green alter ego for a series

00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:20.730
of TV movies, Marvel used that popularity
to shift the spotlight on a few of their other

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:21.730
characters.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:25.620
The second, Trial of the Incredible Hulk,
would use its courtroom setting to introduce

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.960
TV audiences to Marvel's resident attorney,
Daredevil, but the first one pit the Incredible

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.259
Hulk against the Mighty Thor.

00:07:33.259 --> 00:07:38.180
In this case, Thor was played by Eric Kramer,
and while his TV appearance did include Don

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.040
Blake, it wasn't as Thor's alter ego.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.070
Instead, Blake found himself in a sort of
Aladdin situation where Thor was compelled

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:50.669
to do his bidding, leading to a pretty weird
scene where Blake and Thor hung out in a bar

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:54.890
trying to decide whether Thor should take
up a career as a crimefighter.

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:55.919
Clor goes bad

00:07:55.919 --> 00:08:00.830
In 2006, the Marvel Universe was divided in
the Civil War crossover.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:06.380
On one side, Iron Man was pushing for and
eventually enforcing the Superhuman Registration

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:10.920
Act, which would require all superheroes to
sign up with the government for training,

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.759
organization, and oversight.

00:08:12.759 --> 00:08:18.050
On the other, Captain America and a band of
rebels were fighting against the SHRA in the

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:23.060
name of freedom and the slippery slope that
would inevitably lead to giant robots rounding

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:24.120
up mutants.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:29.070
It stands to reason that whichever side got
Marvel's most powerful heroes would have a

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:34.519
pretty big advantage, but there was a problem:
the Hulk was out in the depths of space, and

00:08:34.519 --> 00:08:37.039
Thor was temporarily no longer alive.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:40.009
But Tony Stark didn’t let a problem like
that stop him.

00:08:40.009 --> 00:08:45.140
With a strand of Asgardian hair and some pseudo-science,
he and Reed Richards were able to cook up

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:50.779
the pro-registration side's ultimate weapon:
a cybernetic clone of Thor that was quickly

00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:52.119
dubbed "Clor."

00:08:52.119 --> 00:08:57.670
Given how well cyborgs and clones have gone
for, you know, the entire history of comics,

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:02.170
it won't surprise you that Clor went rogue
at his earliest opportunity and ended up blowing

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:05.240
a hole through a hero called Goliath.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:10.029
While Clor was smashed to bits by Hercules
at the end of Civil War, he was eventually

00:09:10.029 --> 00:09:14.569
rebuilt and christened "Ragnarok," cropping
up in the pages of Dark Avengers before the

00:09:14.569 --> 00:09:20.240
real Thor returned to Midgard and proved that
Ragnarok couldn't hold up to the genuine article.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.069
You should have brought more Thors

00:09:22.069 --> 00:09:26.720
Thor's identity has been pretty malleable
ever since Beta Ray Bill first hefted the

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.990
hammer back in 1983, but recent stories have
seen more people claiming the title than ever

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:32.990
before.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:39.769
A 2013 story saw the arrival of Gorr the God-Butcher,
a cosmic threat so dire that in order to defeat

00:09:39.769 --> 00:09:44.910
him, Thor had to team up with the greatest
warriors he could think of: himself, twice.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:50.339
Together with a young Thor who had yet to
prove himself worthy of Mjolnir and an older,

00:09:50.339 --> 00:09:55.369
one-eyed, one-armed version who had taken
Odin's role as Allfather, they united to defeat

00:09:55.369 --> 00:09:56.369
the God-Butcher.

00:09:56.369 --> 00:09:58.709
It’s a more Gorr Thor Corps!

00:09:58.709 --> 00:10:03.620
In 2014, after the Odinson’s worthiness
was called into question, Mjolnir found a

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:09.379
replacement in the form of a new Thor, one
whose arrival changed the inscription to read

00:10:09.379 --> 00:10:11.269
"if she be worthy."

00:10:11.269 --> 00:10:16.040
This Thor’s identity was initially played
as a mystery, but was eventually revealed

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:21.819
to be the original Thor’s former love interest,
Jane Foster, whose mortal form was dying of

00:10:21.819 --> 00:10:22.959
cancer.

00:10:22.959 --> 00:10:27.309
She proved herself to be more than capable
of living up to her namesake, but during a

00:10:27.309 --> 00:10:32.739
desperate struggle against the monstrous Mangog,
she was only able to defeat him by hurling

00:10:32.739 --> 00:10:38.829
Mjolnir into the sun, apparently destroying
it, and ending her own life in the process.

00:10:38.829 --> 00:10:40.170
And Thor Girl?

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:41.470
We don't talk about Thor Girl.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.029
Thanks for watching!

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:45.660
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:47.410
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

